# Tracked template. `gmake env` concatenates this with the generated
# .env.ports to produce .env, which is gitignored.
# `gmake env-check` fails if this file is newer than .env.
BUILD_SHA=dev
DEPLOY_TARGET=github
AUTO_MERGE=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE REVIEWER'S OWN IDENTITY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Guard 4 wants a review by someone who is not the author. Today the proxy
# reviewer runs as the author's own token, so what it writes is a `review:proxy`
# RECORD -- a note that says "a proxy, NOT a person, accepted this" -- and never
# an APPROVED review, because GitHub refuses to let an author approve their own
# pull request. That refusal is the only thing currently enforcing separation,
# and it is enforcing it by accident.
#
# Giving the reviewer a token of its own makes the separation real: a distinct
# login, a real APPROVED review, and a `gh pr review --approve` that GitHub
# itself rejects if the identities ever collapse back into one. The gating
# requirement stops being simulated and starts being checked by the forge.
#
# LEAVE THIS EMPTY unless you have actually provisioned the second identity.
# An empty value is honest -- change/guard4.sh falls back to the `review:proxy`
# record and says so. A value that is a copy of your own token is WORSE THAN
# EMPTY: it would let the author approve as themselves while every log line
# claims an independent reviewer looked at it. That is the forged-evidence
# failure this repo keeps finding, wearing a costume.
#
# DO NOT NAME THIS `GH_TOKEN`. gh reads GH_TOKEN from the environment and it
# wins over `gh auth`, so a value under that name silently replaces your normal
# identity for EVERY gh call in the shell that sourced .env -- including the
# ones that merge, label and deploy. The reviewer's credential must be visible
# at the call site and nowhere else: it is passed to the single command that
# posts the review, as `REVIEWER_GH_TOKEN=... GH_TOKEN=$REVIEWER_GH_TOKEN gh ...`
# on that one line, and is never exported.
#
# Scopes needed: `repo` (read the diff, write the review). Nothing else --
# no workflow scope, no admin. The reviewer must not be able to merge what it
# approves, or the separation it exists to provide is undone one call later.
#
# See docs/reviewer-identity.org and .claude/agents/jwalsh.md.
REVIEWER_GH_TOKEN=
REVIEWER_LOGIN=
